CUDA graph pointer-update path - mm13, the mechanism that lets batched
passes replay instead of running as thousands of individual launches.

The 2026-08-02 instrumentation campaign ended here: a speculative verify
pass costs ~45ms against a 15.5ms graph-replayed single-token step, and
every candidate for that gap was measured innocent except one - batched
passes NEVER run as CUDA graphs. The mechanism, from source + fleet
phase_us: llama's graph reuse keeps cgraph->uid stable for the 1-token
path, so ggml-cuda skips its property check and replays. A speculative
verify instead rebuilds its graph every round (its scratch KV branch -
seq_rm + seq_cp, the design hybrid-SSM models force, changes the memory
state so llm_graph_result::can_reuse fails), so the uid changes, the
node properties differ, and the stock warmup gate - which demands
consecutive property-stable calls before capturing - never completes.
The pass then executes directly: ~2500 kernel launches, and launch
overhead is the dominant cost under SEV-SNP confidential compute.

But the properties that differ are only the DATA POINTERS: same ops,
same shapes, same strides, same op_params - the KV cache simply lives at
new addresses. That is precisely the case cudaGraphExecUpdate exists
for, and ggml already calls it (ggml_cuda_graph_update_executable) when
a capture is refreshed; the gate just never lets execution reach it.

Change 1: ggml_cuda_graph_update_required() gains an out-param that
classifies a change as topology (node count, op, type, ne, nb,
op_params, src ne/nb) versus data-pointer-only.

Change 2: the warmup gate treats a pointer-only change as capturable -
warmup completes, the graph is re-captured and cudaGraphExecUpdate
patches the new addresses into the existing executable (its existing
failure path already falls back to re-instantiate, so a violated
constraint degrades to stock behavior rather than breaking). Topology
changes still reset warmup exactly as before.

GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_PTR_UPDATE=0 restores stock behavior (config-only
rollback, no rebuild).
